WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.231
Module twelve:

2
00:00:01.231 --> 00:00:04.252
algorithmic trading and artificial intelligence.

3
00:00:04.252 --> 00:00:08.406
What a bot does and does not do, why latency and execution matter,

4
00:00:08.406 --> 00:00:13.190
what machine learning finds in prices, and where language models are useful.

5
00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.291
A bot executes a rule without emotions or fatigue:

6
00:00:17.291 --> 00:00:22.314
it is not afraid to sell after a fall and does not forget to set a stop at night.

7
00:00:22.314 --> 00:00:24.857
But it does not make the rule profitable.

8
00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:29.074
The rule is one link out of five, and most trouble is in the others:

9
00:00:29.074 --> 00:00:31.803
the data arrived late, the exchange refused,

10
00:00:31.803 --> 00:00:35.214
an order filled partly or was repeated after a failure.

11
00:00:36.214 --> 00:00:40.355
While an order travels to the exchange, the price has time to move.

12
00:00:40.355 --> 00:00:44.928
Large players put servers next to the exchange and react in fractions of a

13
00:00:44.928 --> 00:00:48.389
millisecond; competing with them from home is pointless.

14
00:00:48.389 --> 00:00:53.334
A private bot is left with rules that do not care whether they enter now or in a

15
00:00:53.334 --> 00:00:53.766
minute:

16
00:00:53.766 --> 00:00:55.126
daily and hourly ones.

17
00:00:56.126 --> 00:00:57.966
Execution has its own traps.

18
00:00:57.966 --> 00:01:00.792
The exchange limits the number of requests.

19
00:01:00.792 --> 00:01:04.603
A large order fills in parts and at a worse average price.

20
00:01:04.603 --> 00:01:08.152
In crashes, stops fill worse than where they were set.

21
00:01:08.152 --> 00:01:11.175
A good backtest assumes pessimistic execution:

22
00:01:11.175 --> 00:01:15.446
if the idea survives bad execution, it will survive the real one.

23
00:01:16.446 --> 00:01:18.728
Machine learning fits a rule to data:

24
00:01:18.728 --> 00:01:23.600
the model is shown past returns and the right answers, and it looks for a link.

25
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.498
The key to testing it is the walk-forward test:

26
00:01:26.498 --> 00:01:30.198
for each day, the model learns only from the days before it.

27
00:01:31.198 --> 00:01:35.300
The nearest-neighbour method, on the same data it learned from,

28
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.123
guesses 100 percent of days:

29
00:01:37.123 --> 00:01:39.141
it simply remembers the answer.

30
00:01:39.141 --> 00:01:43.113
In a walk-forward test it gets 49 percent, worse than a coin.

31
00:01:43.113 --> 00:01:46.238
The more flexible the model, the bigger the gap.

32
00:01:46.238 --> 00:01:50.014
Under honest testing, all variants stay around 50 percent.

33
00:01:51.014 --> 00:01:53.673
A language model is useful as an assistant:

34
00:01:53.673 --> 00:01:58.250
explaining a term or someone else's code, writing a calculation and tests,

35
00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:03.012
finding a look-ahead bug or a forgotten fee, turning a journal into a report.

36
00:02:03.012 --> 00:02:07.589
But it does not know tomorrow's price, it can confidently invent a number,

37
00:02:07.589 --> 00:02:10.310
and its knowledge ends at its training date.

38
00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:12.109
And security.

39
00:02:12.109 --> 00:02:16.289
Do not give a bot or an agent keys with the right to withdraw funds.

40
00:02:16.289 --> 00:02:20.039
Text from a web page or an email may contain hidden commands.

41
00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:24.342
And check any numbers the model gives you against the original source.

42
00:02:25.342 --> 00:02:26.344
The main points.

43
00:02:26.344 --> 00:02:30.538
A bot executes a rule without emotions but does not make it better.

44
00:02:30.538 --> 00:02:34.231
Machine learning on prices alone almost always finds noise.

45
00:02:34.231 --> 00:02:36.735
And language models are good assistants,

46
00:02:36.735 --> 00:02:40.366
but do not trust them to predict prices or decide for you.
